The return to 120 days for primary residences does not mean that all the other rules governing furnished tourist accommodation have disappeared.

In Nice, change of use remains a central question, especially when a property that is not a primary residence is intended for tourist rental.

Before renting out, several points must therefore be checked:
- the status of the property;
- its location;
- any change-of-use obligations;
- the co-ownership (building) rules;
- the applicable registration and declaration procedures.

Before publishing a listing, it is advisable to check several essential points:
- determine whether the property is a primary or secondary residence;
- check the applicable change-of-use rules;
- complete the necessary registration procedures;
- check the co-ownership rules;
- plan for the tourist tax and reporting obligations;
- prepare the property properly;
- create an attractive, professional listing;
- set up a pricing strategy suited to the Nice market.

This preparation helps avoid many difficulties once the first bookings start coming in.

Yes, provided the property's situation allows tourist rental and the necessary authorisations have been obtained.

In Nice, it is particularly important to check this before publishing the listing and accepting bookings.

Depending on the property's characteristics and location, different rules may apply.

The owner should in particular check:
- the change-of-use rules;
- the declaration or registration procedures;
- the regulations applicable to the area where the property is located;
- the co-ownership rules;
- the tax and reporting obligations related to the rental.

Buying an apartment in Nice to rent it out all year round on Airbnb is very different from occasionally renting out your own primary residence.

As a rental investment, the property will generally be considered a secondary residence or a home dedicated to rental.

Before buying, several points should therefore be studied:
- whether the property can legally be operated as a tourist rental;
- the co-ownership rules;
- local change-of-use rules;
- the property's location;
- the actual rental potential;
- co-ownership charges;
- management and maintenance costs;
- the taxation applicable to the income generated.

An apartment that is very attractive to travellers is not necessarily automatically eligible for tourist rental.

The regulatory check should therefore come before building your profitability plan.

At B&C Conciergerie, we support owners in letting and managing their properties in Nice and on the French Riviera.

We can help you to:
- prepare your property before renting it out;
- create and optimise your Airbnb listing;
- take professional photographs;
- set up a dynamic pricing strategy;
- manage bookings and guests;
- organise cleaning and linen;
- handle check-ins and check-outs;
- coordinate maintenance work;
- provide day-to-day property follow-up.

The regulations adopted in 2026 provide for a quota system for certain change-of-use applications.

The areas concerned include in particular:
- Vieux-Nice (Old Town);
- Riquier – Port – Mont-Boron;
- Centre-Ville (city centre);
- several areas in western Nice.

The existence of a quota means that an application does not automatically entitle you to an authorisation.

The number of authorisations that can be granted may be limited in certain areas.

It is therefore strongly recommended to check the latest situation for the neighbourhood with the relevant City of Nice departments before starting a short-term rental project.

The first step is to determine the property's situation precisely.

The owner should in particular identify:
- whether it is their primary or secondary residence;
- the exact address of the property;
- its current use;
- the regulatory area it falls within;
- any authorisations already attached to the property;
- the provisions of the co-ownership rules.

When a change of use is required, the application must be submitted to the relevant authority following the procedure in force.

It is best to complete this step before starting to operate the property as a tourist rental.

Some mistakes can make a short-term rental project considerably more complicated.

Among the most common:
- publishing a listing before checking the regulations;
- assuming a registration number is automatically enough;
- not distinguishing between primary and secondary residence;
- ignoring the co-ownership rules;
- buying an apartment purely for Airbnb without first checking whether the project is administratively possible;
- relying on outdated regulations found online;
- not checking whether the area is subject to specific rules.

A few checks before publishing the listing can prevent a lot of difficulties later on.

If you are considering buying an apartment to offer as a short-term rental, do not rely solely on its tourist potential.

An apartment close to the Promenade des Anglais, the Port or Vieux-Nice may seem particularly attractive for Airbnb, but its income potential should not be assessed independently of its regulatory situation.

Before investing, it is worth analysing:
- the regulations applicable to the address;
- the co-ownership rules;
- tourist demand in the neighbourhood;
- the guest capacity;
- the property's amenities;
- seasonality;
- average prices charged;
- charges and management costs.

This approach gives a much more realistic view of the project.

At B&C Conciergerie, we support owners in preparing and operating their short-term rentals in Nice and on the French Riviera.

Once your rental project complies with the rules applicable to your property, we can take care of:
- preparing the property;
- creating and optimising the listing;
- professional photographs;
- pricing strategy;
- booking management;
- guest communication;
- cleaning and linen;
- check-ins and check-outs;
- maintenance;
- day-to-day property follow-up.

This is the first step, even before creating your Airbnb listing.

You need to determine whether the property is your primary residence or your secondary residence.

In Nice, since 1 September 2026, a primary residence can be offered as a tourist rental for up to 120 days per calendar year.

For a secondary residence or a property intended mainly for short-term rental, different rules may apply, particularly regarding change of use.

Before getting started, check:
- the status of your property;
- the applicable declaration and registration procedures;
- any change-of-use obligations;
- any specific rules for your area;
- the co-ownership rules.

This step should ideally be completed before investing in furnishing the property or starting to accept bookings.

A pleasant property shouldn't just look good in photos.

It must be functional, clean, comfortable and easy to use.

In Nice, guests may stay only a few days. They need to understand quickly how the property works and easily find everything they need.

Among the essential amenities, make sure to provide:
- comfortable bedding;
- quality bed linen;
- clean towels;
- a properly equipped kitchen;
- reliable Wi-Fi;
- storage space;
- a hairdryer;
- an iron or garment steamer;
- the necessary safety equipment;
- enough tableware for the property's maximum capacity.

Small touches can also considerably improve the guest experience.

Photos are one of the most important elements of an Airbnb listing.

Two similar apartments in the same neighbourhood can perform very differently simply because of the quality of their presentation.

The photos should clearly show:
- the living room;
- the bedrooms;
- the kitchen;
- the bathroom;
- outdoor spaces;
- the view when it is a real asset;
- key amenities;
- what makes the property stand out.

Lighting, framing and the order of the photos also play an essential role.

The property must be perfectly prepared before the shoot: beds made, surfaces cleared, kitchen tidy and no unnecessary personal items visible.

One of the most common mistakes is charging the same price all year round.

In Nice, demand varies greatly depending on the period.

Summer, the Nice Carnival, events, conferences, weekends, school holidays and major events on the French Riviera can all influence prices.

Conversely, some periods call for a more attractive pricing strategy.

Good pricing should take into account:
- the season;
- the day of the week;
- events;
- demand;
- the occupancy rate;
- the lead time before arrival;
- competing properties' prices;
- your apartment's specific features.

Dynamic pricing lets you regularly adjust the price to the market rather than keeping a fixed rate.

Cleaning fees should reflect the real cost of properly resetting the property between two guests.

You need to factor in:
- cleaning;
- making the beds;
- linen;
- inspecting the property;
- restocking consumables where needed.

Inadequate cleaning can quickly lead to poor reviews, even when the apartment is very well located.

On Airbnb, cleanliness remains one of the most important criteria for guests.

Before your first booking, you need to decide how guests will get into the property.

Several solutions exist:
- handing over keys in person;
- a secure key box;
- a smart lock;
- a door code system where the building allows it.

The instructions must be particularly clear.

Photos of the entrance, location of the key box, floor, door number, how the appliances work: the more precise the instructions, the fewer calls you will receive at check-in.

A digital welcome guide can be particularly useful.

A short-term rental inevitably involves a few unexpected events.

A key can be lost. A shower can get blocked. An air conditioner can break down. A guest may arrive late or not understand how to open a door.

You should therefore plan ahead:
- a spare set of keys;
- an emergency contact;
- a professional able to intervene quickly;
- a procedure in case of problems;
- fast communication with guests.

The quality of Airbnb management is often measured more by how a problem is solved than by the complete absence of problems.

Communication starts before arrival and continues until after departure.

It is useful to prepare several automated messages or templates:
- booking confirmation;
- pre-arrival information;
- check-in instructions;
- post-arrival message;
- reminder before departure;
- check-out instructions;
- review request after the stay.

Guests particularly appreciate quick and precise answers.

Good communication directly contributes to a better experience and can help earn positive reviews.

An Airbnb listing shouldn't be created and then left unchanged for months.

You should regularly track:
- the number of bookings;
- the occupancy rate;
- the average nightly rate;
- the periods still available;
- guest reviews;
- performance compared with similar properties;
- changes in demand.

This data lets you gradually adjust your strategy.

Listing an apartment on Airbnb is relatively simple.

Managing it well over time, however, takes time, organisation and a regular presence.

